 "etapas": [
  {
   "etapa": "explicar",
   "piezas": [
    {
     "tipo": "explicacion",
     "titulo": "Your own purpose, or someone else's",
     "texto": [
      "To say **what for** you do something, you have two ways:\n\n- If the person acting is **the same**, use **para + infinitive**: Estudio **para aprender**.\n- If **another person** must act, use **para que + subjunctive**: Te llamo **para que vengas**. Hablo despacio **para que entiendas**.\n\nThat is why you do not say «Te llamo para vengas» (missing **que**) or «Estudio para que aprendo» (the subject is the same).\n\nThe question is **¿para qué?** («what for?»). **¿Por qué?** asks about the cause."
     ],
     "tutora": ""
    },
    {
     "tipo": "gramatica",
     "titulo": "Purpose: para or para que",
     "texto": [
      "Purpose answers the question **¿para qué?** (what for?)\n\n- **Same subject** → **para + infinitive**: Salgo pronto **para llegar** a tiempo.\n- **Different subject** → **para que + subjunctive**: Te explico el plan **para que lo entiendas**.\n\nOther, slightly more formal purpose formulas: **a fin de que**, **con el objetivo de que**, **de modo que** + subjunctive.\n\nCompare **para** and **por**: **para** looks forward (the goal); **por** looks back (the cause). Estudio **para** aprender. Estudio **por** curiosidad."
     ],
